The best versions of Ladebug are those that can be downloaded from
http://www.compaq.com/ladebug

You can find versions for Tru64 v4.* and v5.* systems there and also for Alpha
Linux.  These versions are much later releases than those shipping on the base o/s
kits, with quite a few bugs fixed and enhancements.  If you wait until mid December
2000, the versions we got together this week will be available.  This version for
Alpha Linux will be the first one I am actually comfortable with.  The Tru64
versions have the latest version of our new-a-year-ago GUI.

We (the Compaq Ladebug team) have put a lot of evaluation into the question about
enhancing gdb and enhancing ladebug, which we don't see as competitors but as
alternatives. Which-ever works for you is the one you should use.

Firstly, ladebug and gdb aren't targetting the same users ...

- The Ladebug GUI targets people who don't want to read a debugger manual, ever.
And who don't want lots of training either.

- The Ladebug command line version is really aimed at guru's debugging hard
problems in programs running on Alpha platforms (Tru64 and Linux). We have put a
lot of work into the manual (available at the above web site).  It is .html, and
explicitly aimed at people who know what they are trying to do, but not how to do
it.

- The underlying debugger 'engine' aims at reliable functionality in three areas
where we decided it would be difficult to enhance the open gdb sources

    () Thread support, based on Compaq's pthread libraries
    () C++ and Fortran 90 support
    () The optimized code generated by Compaq's GEM compiler backend with its heavy
Alpha-instruction-set and Compaq .o file format implications.

Each of these is difficult enough to add to Ladebug,  where we can re-engineer the
internals and make massive enhancements.  Dealling with the issues of the size of
changes and getting them back into the open source pools would be very difficult
indeed.

Bevin R Brett
Strategist/developer
Compaq Ladebug team


