> Please explain your comments.  What's wrong with multiple fileset on the
> same domain?  Yes, you need to manage the quota for each fileset
> (chfset, not edquota) but other that that, what are the drawbacks. 
> Addvol increase the risk of failure and longer recovery time, but that
> was not in the discussion.

I'm with the original poster.  Blindly adding more and more filesets to
a big domain is a recipe for disaster.    I don't know how long you've
been using AdvFS, Jack, but my feeling is that while it's improved greatly
in the last couple years, that's no reason to go courting disaster by putting
all your eggs in one basket.

We use AdvFS exclusively on our Digital/Tru64 UNIX boxes, and I have several
current domains that have 3 filesets in them.  I've had to do disaster
recovery on a 3-fileset filedomain because of problems in one of the filesets.
Therein lies the rub -- it's convenient to have multiple filesets share one
filedomain, but if you ever have to do disaster recovery you have to do it
for the entire domain, not just one of the filesets.

Use multiple filesets in one filedomain when it makes sense *and* you can
afford the time/effort to recover the *entire* domain in the event of a
disaster.  Making one huge filedomain and having multiple *unrelated* filesets
within that domain just means that when you lose one (possibly minor) fileset,
you've lost everything.

Tim
