While I love gdb, its support for Tru64 isn't the best.  I *frequently*
run into situations where it doesn't show a full stack trace or it hits
its "heuristic fencepost", even with binaries compiled with `-g' or `-g3'.

ddd truly rocks.  The recent alpha/betas have much better support for
ladebug, too, so now it's almost possible to use ddd on top of ladebug.

BTW, I've said before here and elsewhere that I wish that the Compaq
debugger group would do what HP's debugger group did -- get over the
corporate "not invented here" mindset and adopt and improve the most
popular debugger available (gdb).  HP's port of gdb (wdb, wildebeast)
to HP-UX beats the heck out of any debugger previously available for
HP-UX, *and* the improvements that HP has contributed back into the
gdb core have made gdb better for everyone (but especially for HP-UX).

I will say that I have been impressed by the Compaq debugger group
(and indeed many of the other technical people I've corresponded with
from Compaq).  After posting my "move to gdb" opinion in the past,
I got email from one of the debugger members that had been part of
HP's gdb efforts.  He actually asked me for opinions and "customer
insight" on a number of things, and his comments made it clear that
he was very clueful and his comments also implied that most of his
team was likewise clued in.

Tim
