tru64man@my-deja.com wrote:

> In Chapter 6 in the TruCluster manual it talks about Disk Service--
> "making a disk based application highly available".
>
> I'm a little perplexed. Doesn't the TruCluster environment do that?
>
> Or.
>
> Can someone give me a brief synopsis of disk service in a TruCluster
> environment?

    TruCluster (1.6 - ProductionServer), does have support only for
distributed raw device, and some kind of API's to TruCluster
environment.
    If you have nonCluster application (ie. vi), TruCluster environment
doesnt protect it from crash. But you can make it "highly available".
Ie. If You have nonCluster www server, it stores some data on disks, and
works on one node in the cluster. If that node fails, Your webserver
fails too,... but in few seconds your disks will be remaped to another
cluster node (ip aliasess too), and server goes up. In your webBrowser
it will be only few seconds hole in transfer.
    Sounds great but if you have nonCluster database server, and try to
use disk service to protect your work, you can be dissapointed - if node
fails, database will crash, when db server will be moved to another node
and started, all broken transactions will be lost (server rollbacks all
broken transactions), that isnt very good idea if some kind of balance
sheet was calculated by last two days :-(.
    In that situation You need more sophisticated software, ie Oracle
Paralel Server, it works on two cluster nodes in same time, communicate
beetwin database servers by memory-channel (by TruCluster API), and
works on same raw-disks by distributed raw device.
    Generally if you have application that use blocking devices (ie
standard filesystem files), or/and tcp (flow controlled) sessions,
TruCluster does not ensure uninterrupted failover.

    Read more manual, it is hard lecture, and hard work to separate
real-truth and marketing stuff.

    regards

        Tomasz Gaska





