Hugh,

I remember reading somewhere that, malloc/free combo only release memory back
into re-use by the program. ie you malloc 2 Gb of memory, then free it, it only
gets released back for further mallocs by the same program. I can't remember
where I saw that.

I just read something else, which says that it is designed this way for
application performance methods. As the memory once malloc'ed/free'ed is still
attached to the program future malloc's are much quicker, ie doesn't have to
request memory from the system, system reduce UBC size to allocate the memory to
the program etc... A possible workaround might be to use mmap and munmap for
large allocate large areas of memory, or use shared memory segments. Good luck.

Robert Mulley
