<!doctype html public "-//w3c//dtd html 4.0 transitional//en">
<html>
<head>
   <meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=iso-8859-1">
   <meta name="GENERATOR" content="Mozilla/4.76 [en] (X11; U; OSF1 V5.1 alpha) [Netscape]">
</head>
<body>

<blockquote>
<blockquote>
<blockquote>
<blockquote>
<center><b><font size=+3>Asynchronous I/O Tuning Changes in V5.1</font></b>
<br><b><font size=+1>Tony Verhulst 16 Nov 00</font></b></center>
</blockquote>
</blockquote>
</blockquote>
<b><font size=+2>Before V5.1</font></b>
<p>Before V5.1, AIO data structures were global to the system.&nbsp; That
is, There was one "AIO Completion" array that all tasks competed for (we'll
ignore the per CPU compartmentization option for now).&nbsp; The size of
this global array was controlled by the <b><i>aio_max_num </i></b>parameter
in the <i>rt</i> subsystem (see sysconfig -q rt). It was quite possible
for one task to use the entire global array, thereby starving other tasks
from using AIO.&nbsp; For this reason the <b><i>aio_task_max_num </i></b>rt
configuration parameter was created.&nbsp; When properly set, this parameter
would prevent a single task from using too much of the global resource,
and leave enough for other tasks.
<p><b><font size=+2>NUMA Changes everything.</font></b>
<p>The above worked fine with our existing SMP (<b>S</b>ymetric <b>M</b>ulti
<b>P</b>rocessor)
architectures but in NUMA (<b>N</b>on <b>U</b>niform
<b>M</b>emory <b>A</b>ccess)
architectures, a single global resource like the AIO completion array is
not a good idea for several reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp; If the&nbsp; global data
structure resided on one NUMA node,&nbsp; tasks running on processors on
that node would have rapid access to the data structure because local memory
access is fast.&nbsp; On the other hand,&nbsp; tasks executing on processors
on other nodes would suffer in performance because remote memory access
is much slower.&nbsp; One way around this problem would be to replicate
the data structure so that each NUMA node would have its own structure.&nbsp;
That way all array accesses would be local and, therefore, fast.&nbsp;
The catch (and you knew that there would be a catch, right?) is when you
try to load balance the system.&nbsp; Moving a task from one node to another
would be a bad idea because what would you do with pending AIO operations
in progress?
<p><b><font size=+2>The V5.1 Solution</font></b>
<p>We decided that the best SMP <b>AND </b>NUMA solution would be to completely
eliminate the global AIO completion data structure.&nbsp; When this data
structure disappeared, the <b><i>aio_max_num </i></b>configuration parameter
in the <i>rt</i> subsystem was eliminated because it became meaningless.&nbsp;
What we did was to create an AIO completion array for each task.&nbsp;
This solved our 2 NUMA problems - all array memory accesses became local
and, if the operating system decided to move the task to another NUMA node
(for load balancing purposes) it would be trivial to move the AIO completion
array with it because no one else would be accessing it.
<p>For V5.1, an AIO completion array is allocated for a task when the task
attempts to perform an AIO operation.&nbsp; The size of this array is controlled
by the existing <b><i>aio_task_max_num </i></b>rt configuration parameter.&nbsp;
If a task never uses AIO, no data structure is <i>ever</i> allocated. When
a task using AIO terminates, the memory for the per task AIO data structures
is released back to the system.</blockquote>

</body>
