> Can someone give me analogy or brief explanation of a fileset and
> filedomain in a tru64 system that is using AdvFS?

The "short answer" is:

1) a filedomain is one or more (with AdvFS Utilities) disk drive(s).

2) a fileset is a "dynamic" partition (i.e. you can add or delete them
from a filedomain and you can shrink or grow them, all online).

Because AdvFS is a "logged based file system" it is pretty much "crash
proof" (i.e. no more fsck), although it it recommended that you "verify"
the filedomain "occasionally". (????)

If your using AdvFS, then there is really no reason to "partition" you
disk, except for swap (Tru64 can't swap to a file ... yet)


AdvFS
Taste good, lasts a long time.


