choefgen@abebooks.com writes:

>I turned on one of our DS20 Alpha machines after physically moving it to
>a new location and it no longer boots into the OS.  Because the machine
>was being moved to a different network (10.10.11.* instead of
>10.10.10.10) I changed the basic network settings before shutting it
>down.  The settings I changed were the ip and default gateway, both
>matching the new network.  When I started the machine it hangs after the
>line "nsrd portmap passed".  If you wait for 10 to 15 minutes an error
>message appears :

>rpc.statd: svc_register error for 100024, 1 (udp)

You can often type ^C when booting hangs and whatever offending network
daemon goes away, and usually lets the system up.

If you delete /etc/sm*/*, there's a good chance that will fix things, as
statd is not happy about unreachable systems in /etc/sm.  However,
the svc_register error suggests that portmap is unhappy.  I suspect
you still have missed some configuration thing, but I'd have to think
about it a bit more.

See if you can coax it up, try rpcinfo -p there (or from another
system), try to get tcpdump traces (see the Tru64 FAQ), etc.
