In article <8uelih$f74$1@venus.telepac.pt>, Andr� <andredias@usa.net> wrote:
>Hello All,

Hi there!  We have used an F630 (upgraded from an earlier Filer) for a
few years.  We are using it as a file server for a bunch os Suns and PCs.
Currently about 700GB of disk.  These opinions are mine...

>I would like your opinion about the filers from Network Appliance.

>    Performance?

Used to be good, not so sure now.  Sometimes, good, sometimes seems
very slow.  I think it may be overloaded but NetApp say it should
be OK for the load we have.  We have had a lot of problems over the
last few months which NetApp have been unable to fix.  Their current
theory is that the problems are due to really bad striping of data on our
disks.  Their "fix" for this is to copy all the data off the filer onto
*another* filer so as to sort out the badly organized data!

>    Scalability?

Good.

>    Easy to use?

Good but proprietary (so if you already know Suns/HPs inside out,
it's probably more effort to learn to use NetApp from scratch than
just getting another box you already know how to manage).

>    Backups

Seems good with NDMP.

NetApp tecknical support has been disappointing on several occasions.
Just about every scheduled downtime we have had for the filer has
taken a lot longer than expected and hit problems, even with NetApp
people performing the work.  On one occasion a NetApp engineer 
connected a filer in our server room to a 240V outlet without checking
the voltage setting on the filer PSU... which was set to 110V. *BANG*.
IMHO, Sun tech support is far better (and has to support a much larger
range of products).

>
>Best Regards,
>Andr�

In summary, NetApp filers are quite nice but I'm not planning on buying
another one in the near future... and earlier this year we purchased an
A3500 storage array from Sun rather than another Filer.

