In article <Pine.LNX.4.20.0011101543450.10135-100000@anahata.ma.ic.ac.uk>,
	andy thomas <andy@ic.ac.uk> writes:

>> Increase the value of maxusers in your kernel config file. This is the file
>> /sys/conf/<HOSTNAME>.
>> doconfig -c HOSTNAME
>> cp /vmunix /vmunix.old
>> cp /sys/HOSTNAME/vmunix /
>> reboot system
>> 
>> This will solve your problem.
> 
> Will it? Samba spawns a fresh process for each user's connection but they
> all run as root, not as the user's ID. So even with some 200 samba
> connections open, there are still very few user processes on the system
> (mainly mail, httpd server and so on).

Robert is right (he's Australian, of course he's right!).  Increase maxusers
and you'll be happy.  Or you could increase task-max.  From the DU4.0D docs:

The task-max attribute specifies the maximum number of tasks that can run 
simultaneously.  The default value is 20 + 8 * maxusers.

So if you had the default maxusers of 32, you'd get a maximum of 276 tasks.  
With 200 Samba connections, you could easily reach this limit.

So do what Robert says, you'll be glad you did...
