|> from X.whatever to Z.whatever legally.
|> 
|> 3) I'm not sure about add-on (layered product) licenses, but I do know
|> that licensing NetWorker through DEC/Compaq worked this way -- RTNV was
|> a requirement to legally go from NSR 4.x to NSR 5.x.  My guess is that
|> every single layered product works this way.
|> 
|> 4) If you're an edu customer and have the CSLG (Campus Software License
|> Grant), the CSLG automatically provides RTNV for all products licensed
|> under that program.
|> 
|> 5) Getting RTNV for a particular license generally requires an additional
|> sheet of paper, which you must purchase.  People that know about RTNV
|> generally purchase that bit at the same time as the license it covers,
|> but it can be purchased later, as is often the case for the unsuspecting
|> that discover this issue.
|> 
|> 
|> Again, this info may be completely incorrect.  I'm just telling you what
|> I think I learned, a few years ago when we ran into this problem.

I checked with our software licensing people.  They told me two
useful things:

A) Compaq has an external web page maintained by the corporate 
Software Business Practices group:

  http://www.compaq.com/products/software/info/

The "policies" section has a pointer to "Update Licenses" where
the answer to this particular question can be located.  We should
get this to be easy to find from the Tru64 pages, but I'm not
sure how quickly that will happen.

B) The actual policy is at odds with the answer suggested in 1) above
An update license is needed when the major or minor version number
changes (e.g., 5.1 to 5.2), but not when a letter is added/changed 
(e.g., 4.0f to 4.0g).

