their feelings.  So, never one to shy away from expressing my
opinions, here goes.

We have been using NetApp boxes in our environment for about 5 years,
if I recall correctly.  They have been very reliable for us.  The
performance has been about as good as one can hope with NFS.  The
NVRAM write buffer makes NFS write performance quite respectable.  Do
you pay a premium for NetApp storage?  Yes.  However, I think that in
some cases the premium is worth the stability and simplicity.  They
are just very easy to set up and run, and they generally keep running.

The snapshot feature is also very nice.  It basically saves an image
of the file system metadata, then does a copy-on-write as this data
changes.  So, you can always have one or more snapshots of your file
system available.  It's much faster to copy a file from a snapshot
than restore it from tape backup.  And you can take snapshots much
more frequently than you can backup to tape.  Having said that, we
still do nightly tape backups of our important data.

Is NetApp the perfect solution for all environments?  Doubt it.  EMC
sells lots of boxes, and of course there is Sun.  For some
applications, SCSI or FibreChannel might be the way to go.  In our
environment, we have over 40 RS/6000 SP2 nodes running batch numerical
computation jobs.  We have a couple of terabytes of data that need to
be available at all time to all nodes.  Most of this data is read and
written squentially by jobs that are likely to be bound by CPU, rather
than network or I/O performance.  For large random-access temporary
files, we use IBM SSA disk.  But, for our permanent disk storage,
we have used NetApp.

Hope this helps.

-- 
Greg Wimpey greg.wimpey@westgeo*removetomail*.com.invalid
NOTE:  Opinions expressed are my own, not my employer's.



