Kjetil Torgrim Homme <kjetilho@haey.ifi.uio.no> writes:

> They're expensive, and generally get CPU-starved if you hook up many
> clients.  In my experience, entry level Sun servers (E220R + A1000)
> handle load a lot better, at half the price or less.

Suns are indeed cheaper (at least for us; your prices may vary).
Historically, Suns have scaled better and Netapps have had better
response time and ease-of-management.

However, things are changing.  The current SPECsfs97.v3 scalability
champ is a 16-node Netapp F840 cluster, and if you are thinking about
future growth their DAFS technology looks promising.  See:
<http://www.dafscollaborative.org/>

Sun has not been idle either: they've added a snapshot filesystem
recently (which is one of Netapp's conveniences), and if you want good
low-end performance the Sun StorEdge N8200 has more respectable
benchmark figures than the bad old days when Sun would configure a
separate filesystem on each disk (with no RAID) to boost their
benchmark results.

Those who are concerned about filer performance should read and
understand the benchmarks, and ideally do their own benchmarks.  See:
<http://www.specbench.org/osg/sfs97/results/indexall.html>

> Netapp support have been useless for us, too.  Two years after we
> reported bugs in NFS locking, they still haven't given us an OS which
> works.  This means we have to reboot the filer about once a month to
> clear up such problems.

Wow, you rely on NFS locking?  :-)

My biggest gripe about the Netapp is that their snapshot directories
return bogus inode numbers, which messes up a lot of applications.



