My experience is similar. We have almost entirely Alpha-based
servers (PW 433au, AlphaPC 164SX 533, AlphaServer 400 4/166,
AlphaPC 164 366, AlphaPC 164 432, and the like), all running NTP V4,
including our public st-1 and st-2 NTP servers. The normalized
frequency offset (/etc/ntp.drift) never exceeds 20 ppm,
the usual value is around 10 ppm, one machine has even 0.65 ppm.
(I have some temperature coefficient measurements somewhere,
but can't get them right now - these also are reasonable and even good).

Losing clock interrupts never happens in our servers (normal,
but not excessive server load, including web and imap/pop3 servers).
I heard rumors of lost clock interrupts on Alphas under extreme loads
but I have no experience with that - probably happens to other
platforms as well under extreme conditions.

