In article <iVnIvw3Mfrut@eisner.decus.org>, kaplow_r@eisner.decus.org.mars (Bob Kaplow) writes:
|> In article <8ue2n2$ier$1@nnrp1.deja.com>, mlb@syseca.co.uk writes:
|> > Does anyone know typical drift rate of clocks used in medium-range
|> > Alpha servers from Compaq?  (I realise this may vary with exact machine
|> > spec, but I'm just after an order-of-magnitude).
|> 
|> Probably less than a couple seconds a day.

The Alpha Architecture Reference Manual says that

	The accuracy of the interval clock must be at least
	50 parts per million (ppm).

For some reason, this requirement is in Part II-A, which covers
OpenVMS-related details (section 6.4.5 in the 2nd edition), but
since there is no reason for manufacturing to use different clock
hardware in Tru64 systems, I would assume that this is true of
all Alphas.

50 ppm is 4.32 seconds per day.  My guess is that most of
our systems do better than that, but I'm not sure anyone
can promise that.  Also, it would probably be necessary
to ensure that the system is operating within the temperature
range allowed by its specification, since this can have
an effect on oscillator frequency.

|> IIRC the OS can adjust the fine
|> clock speed, and in fact this is what NTP does to keep the clock in synch.

I don't think the OS can actually adjust the clock speed of
the hardware.  Rather, the OS knows how many microseconds should
have passed between each clock interrupt.  If the clock seems
to be running slightly fast or slow, the value of this increment
can be changed (see "man adjtime") to compensate.

-Jeff


