I'm not an expert on DEC/Compaq licensing (it makes my head spin), but we
encountered this type of problem a while back.  After many, many calls
and emails with various reps withing Compaq and local resellers, it's
now my understanding (i.e.  I could very well be wrong) that

1) if you order a machine and it comes with Tru64 version X.Y, you can
legally upgrade to any newer version of the OS that still has "X" as the
major version, e.g from 4.0 to 4.0g is fine, so is going from 5.0 to 5.1.

2) if you want to upgrade the operating system from version X.Y to Z.W,
you need something called "Rights to New Version", commonly abbreviated RTNV
in the DEC/Compaq documentation.  Once you have this, you can upgrade
from X.whatever to Z.whatever legally.

3) I'm not sure about add-on (layered product) licenses, but I do know
that licensing NetWorker through DEC/Compaq worked this way -- RTNV was
a requirement to legally go from NSR 4.x to NSR 5.x.  My guess is that
every single layered product works this way.

4) If you're an edu customer and have the CSLG (Campus Software License
Grant), the CSLG automatically provides RTNV for all products licensed
under that program.

5) Getting RTNV for a particular license generally requires an additional
sheet of paper, which you must purchase.  People that know about RTNV
generally purchase that bit at the same time as the license it covers,
but it can be purchased later, as is often the case for the unsuspecting
that discover this issue.


Again, this info may be completely incorrect.  I'm just telling you what
I think I learned, a few years ago when we ran into this problem.

When I was researching this before, I had a lot of trouble getting a
straight, decipherable, complete answer out of any of the people within
DEC and also within the resellers I talked to.  Generally that's a sign
that there's a good deal of confusion in their ranks too.  This means
that if someone could come up with a complete, understandable description
of how it works from a legal standpoint, it would be a very useful addition
to the FAQ, online docs areas, etc.

Hope this helps,

Tim
