In article <u8ny9yw2q3v.fsf@beta.uio.no>,
Kjell Andresen  <kjell@usit.uio.no> wrote:
> tru64man@my-deja.com writes:
> 
> > Can someone give me analogy or brief explanation of a fileset and
> > filedomain in a tru64 system that is using AdvFS?
> 
> Domain = collection of physical volumes
> Fileset = logical disk of a domain, mountable

Kjell's synopsis is pretty good, but here's a little more info.  Having
worked with logical volumes on AIX and with Veritas first, AdvFS confused
me at first too.

With traditional LV setups, you often have two "objects":

	Volume Group(s): collection of one or more physical storage devices,
	which form a "pool of available space".

	Logical Volume(s): a fixed-size (but often growable/shrinkable) logical
	chunk of storage.  You generally treat this just like you would a
	disk partition on a "traditional" UNIX system.  Most of the time you
	put a filesystem on top of the logical volume, but you could use the
	LV as a "raw" volume for things like databases.

	Generally, when a logical volume is created, it occupies a fixed set of
	physical sectors on one or more disks within the volume group, sort of
	like how a disk partition on a traditional UNIX system occupies a 
	set of physical sectors all its own.  With a logical volume the sectors
	need not be contiguous, and may span (concatenate) multiple physical
	disks.

With AdvFS, you also have two "objects":

	Domains: collection of one or more physical storage devices, which
	form a "pool of available space".  Very much like volume groups in
	"traditional" LV.

	Filesets: a logical device that is, for want of a better word, a
	"handle" into some amount of storage that comes from the domain it
	is associated with.  Unlike traditional logical volumes, a fileset is
	not "sized", so it immediately has access to all free space within
	the domain.

	If you have two or more filesets in one domain, those filesets are
	(by default) sharing/fighting for all available space in the domain.


With traditional LV, if you have a volume group (foovg) that contains
a single 4 Gig disk and you create two 1 gig logical volumes (lv1, lv2)
from space in that volume group, either volume could be filled without
the other one being affected (since the volumes were sized when created).
If you try to write 1.2 Gig of data to either volume, you know with
100% certainty that it will fail because there just isn't that much space
available, since the volumes have a fixed size that's smaller than the
write you tried.

With AdvFS, if you have a file domain (foo) that contains a single 4 Gig
disk and you create two filesets (fs1, fs2) within that domain, then:

	- filling either fileset means that the other one is "full" too,
	since the volumes are sharing/fighting for the space available in
	the domain (4 Gig, modulo AdvFS overhead).

	- attempting to write 1.2 Gig of data to either fileset may or may
	not work, depending on how much free space is available in the
	domain itself.

	- since all the space is shared between all the filesets, no fileset
	has a particular set of physical disk sectors that it owns.  Data
	for any fileset can be anywhere in the domain.

With AdvFS, you can limit how much space a particular fileset will
use only by establishing an advfs quota for that fileset.  In this
way, you can prevent one fileset from gobbling up all the space in a
particular AdvFS domain.

Note that since Tru64 comes with LSM (a port of Veritas VxVM, a
traditional logical volume system), you may have both a traditional
LVM as well as AdvFS in use on your system, possibly even together
(AdvFS on top of LSM, which we use quite a bit).  Things can get pretty
messy, e.g.

You create an LSM volume group "spacevg", which (initially) consists
of

	dsk16c
	dsk17c
	dsk18c

each a 9 Gig disk.  An LSM volume of ~ 27 Gig (all available space in
the group) called "space-01" is then created.  It's a known/fixed size,
but that size can later be grown/shrunk as long as

	- there's space available to grow it (in this example there is
	not unless more disks are added to that LSM vg)

	- if there's something residing on top of the logical volume,
	whatever that something is needs to know how to grow/shrink
	too!  For example, prior to Tru64 5.1, putting a UFS filesysetm
	on top of an LSM volume meant that although you could technically
	grow the volume, UFS didn't know how to grow, so there wasn't
	any additional space available to it.  5.1 changes this.  I've
	never even tried shrinking a volume, but most things don't
	react well to this.

Now that we have a "space-01", we can create an AdvFS domain "space"
on top of it.

We can then create filesets "foo", "bar", and "baz" within that AdvFS
domain.  The domain has approximately 27 Gig of space available (modulo
space used up first by LSM for its bookkeeping, then space used by
AdvFS for its bookkeeping), all of which is immediately useable by
any one of the three AdvFS filesets.

Now let's say that you need another 27 Gig of space in your "space"
AdvFS domain.  You buy 3 more 9 Gig disks, disks dsk19c through dsk21c.
Your first thought might be to add those three disks to the LSM vg
"spacevg", so it contains

	dsk16c
	dsk17c
	dsk18c
	dsk19c
	dsk20c
	dsk21c

and then grow "space-01" to be 54 Gig in size, expanding the existing
logical volume to fill the additional available physical space.  This
won't work, though, because AdvFS doesn't know how to handle having
one of its storage devices (which may be physical or in this case logical)
change in size.

What you *can* do, however, is to make use of the fact that AdvFS allows
you to add additional storage devices to an existing domain.  This means
that instead of growing the underlying LSM lv onto the 3 new disks, you
should instead create a new logical volume.  You might put those three
disks (19c - 21c) in the LSM vg "spacevg", or you might put them in some
other LSM vg ("spacevg2", for example).  

You then create a new LSM logical volume, which I'll call "space02-01",
which is fixed size, ~ 27 Gig, and occupies all space on dsk19c - dsk21c.
You can use the AdvFS "addvol" command to add the new 27 Gig logical
volume to the "space" AdvFs domain, and suddenly there is 54 Gig available
in that domain.

That's the basics.  Does your head hurt yet?  ;-)  I haven't even touched
on the cool features of AdvFS, such as data migration via addvol/rmvol and
fileset snapshots via "clonefset".

Hope this helps,

Tim
